 9035	/// Whether a declared anchor line contains anything a mutation can change
 9036	/// (M17.5 F-33 / blind pass 1 A04, A06, A10).
 9037	///
 9038	/// Operator rules already existed, but only over a mutation's before/after text
 9039	/// — i.e. only at stage 1b, where evidence exists. At 1a the packet's declared
 9040	/// anchors were checked for POSITION and never for whether the line held any
 9041	/// behaviour. Blind pass 1 walked in and found operators anchored to function
 9042	/// signatures and enum variants.
 9043	///
 9044	/// This rejects exactly one thing: a line that DECLARES rather than does. A
 9045	/// signature has no predicate to invert, no threshold to shift, no crash point
 9046	/// to disable, and no ordering to perturb; whatever the operator, the mutation
 9047	/// cannot be applied there, and a mutant that cannot be applied can never be
 9048	/// killed while still inflating the denominator §3 counts.
 9049	///
 9050	/// It deliberately does NOT try to match operators to line contents. A first
 9051	/// version did, and rejected three legitimate anchors: `NodeFlags(self.0 |
 9052	/// other.0)` is a real inversion site, `if rev == inner.state.head` is a real
 9053	/// staleness comparison, and `for entry in entries.flatten()` is a real
 9054	/// ordering site. Guessing which token an operator needs produces false
 9055	/// accusations against a mutation plan; "is this a declaration" does not.
 9056	/// Whether a mutant is GOOD is the 1b runner's question.
 9057	/// The aliases a `use` line introduces for the symbols it imports:
 9058	/// `use std::fs::{rename as mv, remove_file};` yields `("rename", "mv")`.
